{"id":"a34a50fb-a4f3-43eb-aa2b-3d761ced60a2","object":"chat.completion","created":1785008616,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    edges = case['edges']\\n    refinement = case['refinement']\\n    members = {}\\n    for vertex, group in refinement.items():\\n        members.setdefault(group, []).append(vertex)\\n    agg = {}\\n    for u, v, w in edges:\\n        g_u = refinement[u]\\n        g_v = refinement[v]\\n        key = (g_u, g_v)\\n        agg[key] = agg.get(key, 0) + w\\n    aggregate_edges = [[gu, gv, wt] for (gu, gv), wt in agg.items()]\\n    return {'aggregate_edges': aggregate_edges, 'members': members}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to interpret the task. The output schema has two fields: \"aggregate_edges\" and \"members\". The \"members\" has dynamicKeysFrom case.refinement.values(). That means the keys of the \"members\" object should be the values from the refinement dictionary? Actually, \"dynamicKeysFrom\": \"case.refinement.values()\" means that the keys of the members object should be taken from the values of the refinement dict in the case. In the given fixtures, refinement maps vertices to groups (e.g., \"v0\": \"g0\", etc.). So the values are group names like \"g0\", \"g1\", \"g2\". So the output members object should have keys that are these group names. And each value is an array of strings? The schema says \"additionalProperties\": { \"items\": { \"type\": \"string\" }, \"type\": \"array\" }. So for each group key, the value is an array of strings. Likely we need to group vertices by their refinement group? But the schema says \"dynamicKeysFrom\": \"case.refinement.values()\", which are the group names, not the vertices. So the members object should have keys that are the distinct group names from refinement. The values should be arrays of strings (presumably the vertices that belong to that group). But note: the refinement is given like \"v0\": \"g0\", so the keys of refinement are vertex names, values are group names. So members should have keys = group names, and values = list of vertex names in that group. That seems plausible.\n\nNow \"aggregate_edges\": an array of arrays, each inner array exactly 3 items: [string, string, number]. That suggests edges between groups, aggregated. Likely we need to take the original edges (which are between vertices) and aggregate them into edges between groups. For each original edge (u, v, weight), we map u and v to their groups via refinement. Then we need to create aggregate edges? But the schema only says \"aggregate_edges\" as array of 3-tuple arrays. So we need to produce a list of edges between groups. How to aggregate? Possibly sum weights for edges between the same pair of groups. That is typical in graph coarsening. Also note that the edges might be undirected? The original edges are given as directed? The schema doesn't specify direction, but the edge format is [string, string, number] which could be directed or undirected. Given the problem context (DATA-LEI-02, likely some data lineage or graph processing), we probably need to aggregate directed edges from vertices to groups, preserving direction. So for each edge (u, v, w), we get groups g_u and g_v. Then we produce an aggregate edge (g_u, g_v, w) and sum weights for same (g_u, g_v) pairs. Also, what about self-loops? If u and v are in same group, do we include that edge? Could be included as (g, g, w). But the schema allows same string for first two elements. We'll include them.\n\nAlso, the output must not include any extra fields beyond aggregate_edges and members. The members object keys must be exactly the refinement values (maybe distinct? Yes). Order? Not specified, but JSON object keys order doesn't matter.\n\nThe solve function takes case (payload) and returns the output.\n\nWe need to implement in Python, using only standard library. Use json module for serialization? The function must re